## 1) What home modifications are allowed under CAP/DA?

Under **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Appendix B — “Equipment, Modification and Technology”**, covered home modifications include modifications to a beneficiary’s **primary private residence** if it is:

- owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence **when the modifications are portable**; or
- a rented residence **when portable modifications are allowed by the owner**.

The policy then lists examples of covered home modifications, including:

- **installation, maintenance, and repairs** of:
  - ramps,
  - grab bars,
  - safety rails,
  - handrails;
- **widening doorways or passages** for wheelchair or walker accessibility;
- **emergency egress**, when medically necessary due to physical limitations of the responsible party;
- **bathroom modifications** to improve accessibility, including:
  - toilet,
  - shower and tub, including hand-held showers,
  - sink fixtures or modifications,
  - water faucet controls,
  - plumbing modifications,
  - turnaround space;
- **bedroom modifications** to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- **kitchen modifications** to improve accessibility, including:
  - cabinets,
  - sink fixtures or modifications,
  - water faucet controls,
  - related plumbing modifications,
  - turnaround space;
- **floor coverings** for ease of ambulation;
- **door handle replacements**;
- **other modifications outside these general categories approved by the State Medicaid Agency**; and
- **replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space** when removed, dismantled, or damaged during an approved modification.

See **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Appendix B — “Equipment, Modification and Technology”**, effective Sept. 1, 2025. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## 2) What must the MDT provide as documentation?

The clearest documentation list I found is in **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Section 5.9.1 — “Equipment, Modification, Technology and Supplies.”** That section says the request must include:

- **A recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the home modification requested, and **the recommendation must state the cost** of the item the beneficiary requires;
- **a plan** for how the beneficiary and family will be trained on the use of the equipment, with the training:
  - documented by the case manager as completed, and
  - signed by the beneficiary or responsible party;
- a **statement of need identified in the service plan**;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, **when applicable**;
- **shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request;
- **other information as required for the specific equipment requested**;
- when quotes are required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, **NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required**; and
- **NC Medicaid or its designee determines the appropriate professional(s)** who must make written recommendations when special circumstances are presented.

See **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Section 5.9.1 — “Equipment, Modification, Technology and Supplies”**, effective Sept. 1, 2025. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## 3) What specifically does the policy say about the MDT’s role?

From the CAP/DA service definition text in **Appendix B — “Equipment, Modification and Technology,”** I was able to confirm that the file also requires a:

- **comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification needs; and
- **copy of the physician’s order, when applicable**.

